<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James II of England</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1633–1701</span> · Also known as James VII of Scotland, James, Duke of York</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, also James VII of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, was King of England, Scotland and Ireland from 1685 to 1688. As Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york-maine">York</a> he received New Netherland from his brother Charles II in 1664 and it was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>; as king he created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and absolutism led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a>, and his defeat in Ireland in 1690 ended his hopes of return.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-england.jpg" alt="James II of England" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: School of Peter Lely · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_II_by_Peter_Lely.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
